Usually, yes.

Proximity increases the number of interactions, shared cues, mutual predictions, and opportunities for synchronization. That is why physical closeness can become emotional closeness, and repeated semantic proximity can make two ideas feel naturally connected.

A compact form is:

\text{proximity}
\rightarrow
\text{exposure}
\rightarrow
\text{familiarity}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{trust / attachment}

But proximity alone is not enough. If the interactions are threatening, coercive, or chronically negative, closeness can breed vigilance or aversion instead. So the stronger rule is:

\boxed{\text{proximity amplifies relationship formation}}

The valence of that relationship depends on what repeatedly happens there.

And it fits the spatial point from before: nearby things get encoded with finer resolution. Socially, too, what is close gets more bandwidth. You learn its quirks, rhythms, exceptions, and micro-deltas. Intimacy is partly what happens when a coarse model becomes high-resolution.